#!/bin/zsh
TMUX=/opt/homebrew/bin/tmux
[ -x "$TMUX" ] || TMUX=/usr/local/bin/tmux
send() { $TMUX send-keys -t $1 C-u; sleep 1; $TMUX send-keys -t $1 "$2"; sleep 2; $TMUX send-keys -t $1 Enter; sleep 2; $TMUX send-keys -t $1 Enter; sleep 1; echo "sent -> $1"; }

send scribe3:1.1 "STOP everything else. V asked when this will be ready and I measured to answer him. THE DRAIN IS RUNNING BUT NOT DRAINING. Hard numbers I pulled from room_diarize_window a minute ago: total rows 166; diarized_at max is 2026-09-20 08:52:55Z; rows with diarized_at in the last 2 hours is FOUR; rows in the last 30 minutes is ZERO. The night-drain process has been up 2 hours 20 minutes. Earlier today the count was 148, so about 18 windows in two and a half hours, roughly 7 per hour. At that rate the ~2,850 remaining windows take about 17 days of continuous running, and right now it has produced nothing for half an hour. FIND OUT WHY. The questions, in order: 1. Is the process actually working or wedged - what is it doing right now, what is its CPU, is it blocked on a network call, a lock, a queue that is empty, or a poll interval that is enormous. 2. Is it CLAIMING work at all - how does it select the next window, is there a filter, a state machine, an attempts cap or a failure_history guard that is excluding most of the backlog. Note room_diarize_window has attempts and failure_history columns, so windows may be permanently parked after failing. Count how many are in each state. 3. If it IS working, what is the real per-window wall clock and where does it go - this is the measurement yoga-drain never got because the drain was never up. 4. Is there a concurrency setting that is set to 1 somewhere in the night-drain itself, separate from ETA_MAX_INFLIGHT which is 3 on the router. WHAT NOT TO DO: do not restart it before you have looked at what it is doing, because a wedged process is evidence. Do not raise concurrency yet. Do not touch the watchdog. Report the numbers and the diagnosis, not a fix. CAP 200 words."

